===Multiple accounts, apparent contradictions===
[[File:Polo Bridle Pelham.JPG|thumb|upright|Apparent contradiction: Tolkien stated that Elves rode without saddle or harness of any kind, but gives [[Glorfindel]]'s horse both [[Bit (horse)|bit]] and [[bridle]] (modern bit and bridle shown).]]

Scholars such as Peri Sipahi note that the use of multiple accounts is introduced in the Prologue to ''The Lord of the Rings'', where the narrator explains that "many of their traditions, up to that time still mainly [[Oral tradition|oral]], were collected and written down",<ref group=T>{{harvnb|Tolkien|1954a}}, "Prologue", "Note on the Shire Records"</ref>{{sfn|Sipahi|2016|pp=93-95}} and then details the various copies, redactions, and translations that were made of the fictive ''[[Red Book of Westmarch]]''. ''Beowulf'' is similarly written as if its audience knew of the historic characters already.{{sfn|Sipahi|2016|pp=93-95}}{{sfn|Drout|Hitotsubashi|Scavera|2014|pp=167–211}} The ''[[Poetic Edda]]'' is a compilation of numerous older sources.{{sfn|Sipahi|2016|pp=93-95}}

Apparent contradictions, Drout notes, tend to give readers the impression of a real and complex history, since they may assume that an omniscient author can make a fictional story wholly consistent. Among the examples he gives are that Tolkien stated that Elves rode without either a [[saddle]] or a harness, but the Elf-lord [[Glorfindel]]'s horse is described as having both [[Bit (horse)|bit]] and [[bridle]], and Glorfindel says he will shorten his horse's [[stirrup]]s for the Hobbit [[Frodo Baggins|Frodo]].<ref group=T>{{harvnb|Tolkien|1954a}}, book 1, ch. 12 "Flight to the Ford"</ref>{{efn|In a letter written after the publication of ''The Lord of the Rings'', Tolkien writes that "actually ''bridle'' was casually and carelessly used for what I suppose should have been called a ''headstall''... Glorfindel's horse would have an ornamental ''headstall'', carrying a plume, and with the straps studded with jewels and small bells; but Glor[findel] would certainly not use a ''bit''. I will change ''bridle and bit'' to ''headstall''."<ref group=T>{{harvnb|Carpenter|2023|loc=letter 211 to Rhona Beare, 14 October 1958 }}</ref>}} In another case, Tolkien intentionally did not edit away the contradiction between [[Tom Bombadil]]'s claim that he was "Eldest ... here before the river and the trees; Tom remembers the first raindrop and the first acorn",<ref group=T>{{harvnb|Tolkien|1954a}}, book 1, ch. 7 "In the House of Tom Bombadil"</ref> and Gandalf's description of the [[Ent]] [[Treebeard]] that "Treebeard is Fangorn, the guardian of the forest; he is the oldest of the Ents, the oldest living thing that still walks beneath the Sun upon this Middle-earth".<ref group=T>{{harvnb|Tolkien|1954}}, book 3, ch. 5, "The White Rider"</ref>{{sfn|Drout|Hitotsubashi|Scavera|2014|pp=167–211}}

===Varying styles===
{{further|Poetry in The Lord of the Rings}}

The characters from each part of Middle-earth speak and act in a way that is characteristic of that place, as with the [[Rohirrim]].{{sfn|Sipahi|2016|pp=93-95}} Sipahi notes, too, that all four factors tend to occur together, again as seen in the account of the Rohirrim. In addition, their language and names—all taken from [[Old English]]—lend further depth by linking back to the [[medieval]] period in the real primary world.{{sfn|Sipahi|2016|pp=93-95}}

== Reception ==

The critic and experimental novelist [[Christine Brooke-Rose]] attacked "the histories and [[Tolkien's Middle-earth family trees|genealogies]]" as not "''in the least'' necessary to the narrative",{{sfn|Brook-Rose|1981|p=247}} thus demonstrating in Shippey's view her ignorance of Tolkien's creation of depth. He notes that she guessed wrongly that Tolkien would have translated all the "runic and other messages inside the narrative",{{sfn|Brook-Rose|1981|p=247}} as, he suggests, almost all other authors would have done, but that Tolkien did not, as he saw [[Poetry in The Lord of the Rings#Glimpses of another world|a value in the sound of untranslated language]].{{sfn|Shippey|2005|pp=364–365, where Shippey points also to pp. 129, 203, and notes 5 and 10 on pp. 441–441}}

Later fantasy authors such as [[Ursula K. Le Guin]] made use of the device of giving pseudo-references to create depth; in the ''[[Earthsea]]'' novels, she alluded to tales of Elfarran, Morred, and the Firelord, which she wrote many years later.{{sfn|Drout|Hitotsubashi|Scavera|2014|pp=167–211}} The scholar Katherine Sas writes that [[J. K. Rowling]] scaled down Tolkien's impression of depth but applied all four factors involved to her book ''[[Harry Potter and the Prisoner of Azkaban]]''.{{sfn|Sas|2019|loc=Article 9}}

